Task vs Thread in C#
Imagine you are working in a restaurant.
- Thread = A waiter who performs work.
- Task = An order/work that needs to be completed.
One waiter can handle multiple orders over time. Similarly, a thread can execute multiple tasks.




















6










Simple code example
// Task
Task.Run(() =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("Task is running");
});

// Thread
Thread t = new Thread(() =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("Thread is running");
});

t.Start();


Difference
Task	Thread
Represents an operation	Represents an actual execution thread
Managed by Task Scheduler	More direct control over execution
Supports async/await	Does not directly support async/await
Usually preferred for application-level asynchronous work	Used when direct thread control is needed

Interview answer:

"Task represents work that needs to be executed, whereas Thread is an actual execution unit that executes the work. Task is generally preferred in modern .NET applications because it supports async/await and is managed efficiently by the Task Scheduler."
